# CKA Scenario 19: High-Availability Control Plane (`ha-cluster`)

Hands-on training guide and operational verification procedures for deploying, maintaining, and verifying a **Highly
Available Kubernetes Control Plane** using stacked etcd, co-located **HAProxy** load balancing, and **Keepalived VRRP**
virtual IP migration.

---

## 1. Exam Relevance & Competency Breakdown

High Availability control plane management is a core objective in the **Cluster Architecture, Installation &
Configuration** domain (25% scoring weight) of the Certified Kubernetes Administrator (CKA) exam:

| CKA Exam Competency | Real-World Application | Scenario Drill |
| :--- | :--- | :--- |
| **Stacked Control Plane Topology** | Multi-master apiserver + co-located etcd members | `01-verify-vip.sh` |
| **Control Plane Endpoint & Certs** | `--control-plane-endpoint` and `--upload-certs` | Step 1–2 in README |
| **Multi-Master Node Join** | `kubeadm join ... --control-plane --certificate-key ...` | Step 3 in README |
| **etcd Quorum & Health** | `etcdctl endpoint health` across all members | `02-etcd-quorum.sh` |
| **Failover Resilience** | VRRP VIP migration and surviving member continuity | `03-simulate-failover.sh` |
| **HA Rolling Upgrades** | Sequential CP upgrades, etcd quorum, `kubeadm upgrade node` | Step 5 & `04-ha-upgrade-sequence.sh` |

